#### environment
#
# Every variant, not just the bare name: `.env` alone leaves `.env.local` and
# `.env.production` to be committed by whoever writes one. An example file is
# named back in, because it carries the variable names and none of their
# values, and a project built from this template is expected to commit one.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
node_modules/
.eslintcache
# Ignore the contents, not the directory itself — git cannot re-include a
# path whose parent directory is excluded, so `.scratch/` here would make
# the `!` line below dead and leave `.scratch/` absent from a fresh clone.
.scratch/*
!.scratch/directory-keeper

#### keys and certificates
#
# Nothing here is authored in this repository, and the entries exist for what
# gets built inside one made from it — a TLS certificate dropped in to test
# against, a deploy key, a keystore. A secret committed once is committed for
# as long as the history lives, so the patterns are here before the first
# project needs them rather than after one of them leaks.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519

#### implementation repositories, fetched or adopted by /hora-setup
/*-backend*/
/*-frontend*/

# A repository adopted under its own directory name matches neither pattern
# above, so /hora-setup appends one literal entry per declared `Directory`
# here. Left out, the whole repository gets committed into this one, and
# nothing says so until somebody reads `git status`.

#### one machine's own Claude Code settings
#
# The permissions somebody accepted on their own machine, and whatever else
# they configured locally. It is the settings file that carries no `.local`
# that a repository shares; this one answers to one checkout and nothing else,
# and committing it hands everyone else a set of allowances they never granted.
/.claude/settings.local.json

#### the kit equipped by postinstall, from @openreachtech/hora and
#### @openreachtech/hora-skills (regenerated, not authored here)
#
# Ignore the contents of both payload directories, and name this repository's
# own entries back in, one literal line each. What the two packages distribute
# changes with every release, so a pattern written against today's names goes
# stale without saying so — and a denylist that stops matching says nothing
# when it stops: every equipped entry would simply start being committed here.
# An allowlist cannot fail that way. Nothing is named back in today, because
# this repository authors no skill and no agent of its own.
/.claude/agents/*
/.claude/skills/*

# The two equip manifests, written by the install commands. These are the only
# entries under .hora/ that are ignored: everything else there is the run's own
# record, and `git log .hora/` is the history of what ran.
/.hora/equip-core.json
/.hora/equip-skills.json

